<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Durrës</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dyrrhachium, Epidamnos, Durazzo, Epidamnus, Dyrrachium</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Durrës is a port city on the Adriatic coast of Albania, founded around 627 BCE by Greek colonists from Corinth and Corcyra under the name Epidamnos. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> called it Dyrrhachium and made it the starting point of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-egnatia">Via Egnatia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. Contested by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, Normans, Venetians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, it served as Albania's capital from 1914 to 1920 and is now its second city.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/durres.jpg" alt="Durrës" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attila Terbócs · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amphitheatre_of_Durr%C3%ABs_2013-09_005.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
